package components

// ListItem renders one <li> in the suggestion-list/candidate-list/
// assignment-list family - NOT plan-list, which looks like the same
// family (same shared row styling in components/list-item.css) but isn't:
// a plan-list row's first child is an <a> (the row's own link), and
// components/list-item.css's bold/flex-grow rule only ever targets
// ".suggestion-list li/.candidate-row/.assignment-list li > span:first-
// child" - plan-list was deliberately left off that selector, so its
// rows get their emphasis from the link itself instead. The three lists
// this does cover share an implicit contract no class name spells out:
// the *first* child of the <li> must be a <span> holding the row's
// title, with everything else (badges, muted meta, forms) following it.
// This component makes that first-span-is-the-title rule structural
// instead of a convention every call site has to remember to follow by
// hand. list-item.css gives that title span flex:1 1 auto, so it grows to
// fill whatever width its trailing siblings (badges, forms) don't need,
// keeping title + trailing content together on the row's first line with
// the trailing content shrink-to-content and right-aligned - the one
// exception is a span with the "arrangement-info" class (meter/bpm/key -
// set_builder.templ's songCandidateMeta), which list-item.css forces onto
// its own full-width line below, the same title-then-meta-underneath
// shape set_builder.templ's own .song-meta uses for a set song's own row.
// class carries the optional "candidate-row" modifier (suggestion/
// assignment rows leave it ""); attrs carries whatever data-*/onclick
// attributes that particular row needs (assignment rows' data-row-id+
// onclick for selectPersonRow()) - nil where a row needs none. Candidate
// rows themselves no longer carry any (search/role-filtering happens
// server-side now - see CandidateSearchResults, set_builder.templ).
templ ListItem(class string, attrs templ.Attributes, title string) {
	<li class={ class } { attrs... }>
		<span>{ title }</span>
		{ children... }
	</li>
}
